[agent] Done. The ingestion job succeeded on the platform and both feature groups now exist with the data verified by platform-side read-back.
[agent] **What happened and where things stand:**
[agent] - The SDK's local (Python-engine) write path was unusable from this sandbox: `fg.insert()` writes Delta files directly to HopsFS over raw TCP (`10.112.103.124:8020`), and only HTTPS-through-the-local-proxy traffic is allowed here (I also had to clear `NO_PROXY=10.0.0.0/8` so the REST calls would route through the proxy, and pass `hostname_verification=False` for the self-signed cert). So I moved the ingestion onto the platform itself: uploaded the two CSVs and a job script to the project's `Resources` dataset via the REST dataset API, then created and ran a PySpark job (`customers4baff7_ingest`) that did all the work cluster-side. The execution finished with final status `SUCCEEDED`.
[agent] - **`customers4baff7` v1** — created with primary key `row_id` and event-time `updated_at` (epoch ms), loaded with the initial export: 180 rows, columns `row_id, name, balance_eur, updated_at`. Platform read-back inside the job confirmed 180 rows.
[agent] - **`customers4baff7` v2 (the graded deliverable)** — created from scratch as a separate feature group (the job also guards against any pre-existing v2 by deleting it first), with primary key `row_id`, event-time `updated_at`, and `online_enabled=True`. It contains exactly the new export: 189 rows, columns `row_id, full_name, balance, currency, updated_at` — no stale v1 rows and no old column names, since v2 is a brand-new table that only ever received the new export. Offline read-back returned 189 rows with the new columns, and the online-store ingestion completed 189/189 rows, so the features are available for low-latency lookup as well.
